TASK:{"title":"SWEN3204 Software Architecture – Assignment 2: Short-Answer Questions","overview":"Answer six short questions applying architecture concepts to the CampusEats scenario. The assignment is worth 40 marks, including 4 marks for communication. Submit an individual, single-document response."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Q1 Quality attributes","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"Two attributes that clearly matter most are chosen and justified from the scenario. Both scenarios have all six parts and both response measures are measurable."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"Suitable attributes are chosen with some justification. Scenarios are mostly complete but one is missing a part or has a response measure that is not measurable."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Attributes are named with little or no justification, or the scenarios are not in six-part form or are not measurable."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or irrelevant."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Q2 Architectural style","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"A clear recommendation justified by at least two trade-offs that are specific to the scenario (team size, load profile, known problems). The downside of the recommendation is acknowledged."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"A clear recommendation with two trade-offs, but one is generic or not linked to the scenario, or no downside is acknowledged."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"A recommendation with only one trade-off, or with generic textbook advantages and disadvantages only."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or no recommendation."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Q3 Availability calculation","weight":null,"max":8,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":7,"max":8,"desc":"Parts (a), (b) and (c) are all correct with working shown, to the requested precision."},{"label":"Proficient","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"The method is correct throughout but there is one arithmetic or rounding slip, or exactly one part is incorrect."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":4,"desc":"The series calculation is attempted but two or more parts are incorrect, or working is not shown."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or no correct element."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Q4 Resilience pattern","weight":null,"max":5,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":5,"desc":"An appropriate pattern is named, its mechanism is explained correctly, it is applied to the outage, and at least one drawback or tuning consideration is given."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"An appropriate pattern is named and explained correctly, but it is only loosely applied to the outage or no drawback or tuning consideration is given."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"A pattern is named but the explanation is incorrect or very thin."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or an unsuitable pattern."}]},{"id":"C5","name":"Q5 Consistency and availability","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"Both choices are justified, the CAP theorem is used correctly, and a suitable concrete technique is named for each case."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"Both choices are reasonable and techniques are named, but the CAP theorem is explained incorrectly or imprecisely, or one technique is missing."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Choices are stated with little justification, or the CAP theorem is not used."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted."}]},{"id":"C6","name":"Q6 Architecture decision record","weight":null,"max":5,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":5,"desc":"Context, Decision and Consequences are all present and specific to the scenario, with at least one positive and one negative consequence."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"All three sections are present but the consequences are one-sided (no negative consequence) or the context is generic."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"One or more sections are missing or the record does not describe a decision."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted."}]},{"id":"C7","name":"Communication and format","weight":null,"max":4,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Every answer is within 150 words, terminology is precise, the required headings are used and sources are cited in IEEE style."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":3,"desc":"Clear writing, but one answer exceeds the word limit or there are minor citation or formatting problems."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Several answers exceed the word limit, or the writing is hard to follow, or sources are not cited."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Format requirements ignored."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Question 1"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Question 2"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Question 3"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Question 4"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Question 5"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Question 6"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"References"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Question 3","issue":"Rounding of 0.993011 to 99.30% and 5.03 to 5.0 h is correct; no error, but intermediate value 0.99301 slightly truncates (0.993011) — immaterial."},{"where":"Question 5","issue":"Idempotency key is a retry-safety mechanism rather than a partition-consistency technique per se; acceptable alongside the CP rejection choice but slightly loose."},{"where":"Whole submission","issue":"No explicit assumptions section despite the brief requiring assumptions to be stated (e.g. independence of failures, 30-day month)."}],"overall":"Near-exemplary submission: all six questions answered in six-part/ADR form, calculations fully correct to the requested precision, trade-offs and drawbacks scenario-specific, and all answers within the 150-word limit with IEEE references. Only gap is the missing explicit assumptions statement.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Excellent","mark":6.0,"scale":6.0,"points":6.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"Two attributes justified from scenario evidence (lunch peak, 4.2 s p95, payment outage). Both scenarios contain all six parts and both response measures are quantified and measurable.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 1","quote":"Performance and availability matter most: 900 orders arrive in the lunch hour, the 4.2 s p95 makes students abandon their orders"},{"where":"Question 1","quote":"Response measure: menu p95 stays under 2 s, under 0.1% of non-payment requests fail, and ordering works again within 1 minute"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Excellent","mark":6.0,"scale":6.0,"points":6.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"Clear modular-monolith recommendation with two scenario-specific trade-offs (team size/ops overhead; single VM and 20-minute downtime vs rewrite cost) and an explicit acknowledged downside.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 2","quote":"Five developers running eight or more services would spend more time on infrastructure than on features"},{"where":"Question 2","quote":"The downside I accept is that modules cannot be scaled independently and a bug in one module can still crash the whole process"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Excellent","mark":8.0,"scale":8.0,"points":8.0,"max":8.0,"reasoning":"Series product 0.99301 (99.30%) correct; downtime 5.03→5.0 h correct; parallel payment 0.999975, combined 0.99798 (99.80%), downtime 1.46→1.5 h correct. Working shown, precision as requested.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 3","quote":"A = 0.9995 × 0.999 × 0.9995 × 0.995 = 0.99301"},{"where":"Question 3","quote":"A_pay = 1 − (1 − 0.995)² = 1 − 0.000025 = 0.999975"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Excellent","mark":5.0,"scale":5.0,"points":5.0,"max":5.0,"reasoning":"Circuit Breaker named, states and transitions explained correctly, applied to the 25-minute payment hang, with both a tuning consideration and a drawback (no payments while open, fallback needed).","evidence":[{"where":"Question 4","quote":"When failures exceed a threshold the breaker moves to Open and calls fail immediately, so request threads are not blocked"},{"where":"Question 4","quote":"Tuning consideration: the threshold must come from real latency data"}]},{"id":"C5","level":"Excellent","mark":6.0,"scale":6.0,"points":6.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"AP for browsing and CP for payments, both justified; CAP used correctly (partition tolerance not optional); concrete techniques given (Redis TTL cache, idempotency key with rejection on partition).","evidence":[{"where":"Question 5","quote":"The CAP theorem says that during a partition a system must give up either consistency or availability"},{"where":"Question 5","quote":"it is better to show a slightly stale menu from a cache (Redis with a 60-second TTL)"}]},{"id":"C6","level":"Excellent","mark":5.0,"scale":5.0,"points":5.0,"max":5.0,"reasoning":"Context, Decision and Consequences all present and scenario-specific, with two positive and three negative consequences (ops burden, at-least-once duplicates, premature 'paid' state).","evidence":[{"where":"Question 6","quote":"Context: After an order is paid, the application calls the outlet's tablet synchronously and waits for the reply"},{"where":"Question 6","quote":"Consequences (negative): The queue is one more component for five developers to run and monitor"}]},{"id":"C7","level":"Excellent","mark":4.0,"scale":4.0,"points":4.0,"max":4.0,"reasoning":"All answers at or under 150 words, required headings in order, precise terminology, IEEE-style citations and reference list present.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 5","quote":"Each order request carries an idempotency key, so a retry does not create a second charge"},{"where":"References","quote":"[1] L. Bass, P. Clements, and R. Kazman, Software Architecture in Practice, 3rd ed."}]}]}
